{
  "image_name": "7dc65de98eebc8ec9168dad1c8fdeaf9.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how magnetic anisotropy in thin ferromagnetic elements changes the preferred magnetization direction and switching behavior, and links perpendicular anisotropy to an interface effect in CoFeB/MgO. On the left, in-plane anisotropy is illustrated by a flat elliptical disk whose easy axis lies along the film plane, while perpendicular anisotropy is shown by a cylindrical element whose magnetization points out of plane. The top-down views and arrows emphasize the different anisotropy fields and stable directions.\n\nThe middle rows compare reversal pathways. For in-plane magnets, thermally activated switching appears to follow a broader rotational path across the ellipse, whereas perpendicular magnets switch between up and down states through a simpler intermediate state. Spin-transfer-torque switching is also contrasted: the in-plane case shows a more extended precessional trajectory, while the perpendicular case is more symmetric and compact, implying a simpler switching landscape and different critical current scaling. The bottom sketches indicate different stray-field patterns around the two geometries.\n\nOn the right, the origin of interface anisotropy is depicted at the CoFeB/MgO boundary. A crystal schematic and orbital shapes suggest hybridization between Fe d orbitals and O p orbitals at the interface, which appears to favor out-of-plane magnetization.",
